course: french
lesson: 3
title: Accents: é, è, ê, ç and friends
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: French letters sometimes wear little hats, lines and tails, called accents, and they are part of the spelling, just like the dot on an i.
explain: An accent can change the sound: café without its é would be read "kaf", and sale (dirty) and salé (salty) are two different words.
explain: An accent can also change only the meaning: ou means "or" and où means "where", and they sound exactly the same.
explain: The little hat ê often marks a lost s from long ago, so forêt was once "forest", and fête was once "feste" (a feast).
explain: Leave an accent off in a French test and you lose a mark, just as you would for writing "frend" instead of "friend" in English.
fact: The accent aigu (é) leans up to the right and makes e say "ay": le café is "ka-FAY", l'école (school) is "lay-KOL".
fact: The accent grave (è) leans down to the right and makes e say "eh", as in "bed": la mère (mother) is "MAIR".
fact: la mère (mother), la mer (sea) and le maire (mayor) all sound like "MAIR" but are spelled differently and mean different things.
fact: The accent circonflexe is a little hat (ê, â, î, ô, û); it often stands where an s used to be: forêt (forest), hôpital (hospital), fête (feast), île (isle).
fact: The cédille is a little tail under c (ç); it makes c say "s" before a, o or u: le garçon (boy) is "gar-SON", ça (that) is "SA".
fact: Without its cédille, garçon would be read "gar-KON", because c before a, o or u says "k".
fact: a without an accent means "has": il a un chat (he has a cat); à with an accent means to, at or in: il va à Paris (he is going to Paris). They sound the same.
fact: ou means "or" and où means "where": Thé ou café ? (tea or coffee?), Où est le chat ? (where is the cat?).
fact: The tréma is two dots (ë, ï); it tells you to say two vowels separately: Noël is "no-EL", le maïs (sweetcorn) is "ma-EES".
fact: In good French writing, capitals keep their accents: ÉCOLE, À BIENTÔT.
fact: sale (no accent) means dirty and sounds like "SAL"; salé means salty and sounds like "sa-LAY".
fact: The names: aigu means sharp, grave means low, circonflexe means bent around; cédille is "say-DEE-y" and tréma is "tray-MA".
fact: Ce n'est pas grave ! means "It doesn't matter!" or "No big deal!"; here grave means serious and has nothing to do with the accent.
fact: Slang here: "boss" (Liverpool) means great; "wee" (Scotland) means small; "heaps" (Australia) means very; "lush" (Wales) means lovely; "peng" (London) means great-looking; "them accents is" is broken grammar for "those accents are".
exercise: Poppy writes cafe with no accent in her French test. Why does she lose a mark? | Accents are part of the spelling in French | The teacher wanted it written in capitals | cafe is an English word, so it is banned | French words must always end in an accent
exercise: What sound does é make? | "ay", as in café | "ee", as in "see" | silent, like a final s | "uh", as in "the"
exercise: Which three words all sound like "MAIR"? | la mère, la mer and le maire | la mère, la mare and le mort | la mer, la merci and le mardi | la mère, le mari and la mairie
exercise: What does la mer mean? | the sea | the mother | the mayor | the town hall
exercise: The ê in forêt often stands for which lost letter? | s | t | h | e
exercise: Which English word is hiding in the French word bête? | beast, because the hat marks a lost s | bet, because the e was doubled | bite, because the t means teeth | beat, because the hat means a drum
exercise: What does the cédille do in garçon? | It makes the c say "s" | It makes the c silent | It makes the c say "k" | It makes the c say "ch"
exercise: How would garcon sound without its cédille? | "gar-KON" | "gar-SON" | "gar-CHON" | "gar-ZHON"
exercise: Which sentence needs à with an accent? | Il va _ Paris. (He is going to Paris.) | Il _ un chien. (He has a dog.) | Elle _ une sœur. (She has a sister.) | Il _ dix ans. (He is ten.)
exercise: What does Où est la gare ? mean? | Where is the station? | Or is the station? | Is the station open? | Who is at the station?
exercise: What does Thé ou café ? mean? | Tea or coffee? | Where is the tea and coffee? | Tea and coffee? | Is there tea in the café?
exercise: What do the two dots in Noël tell you? | Say the o and the e as two separate sounds | Say the e twice as long as normal | The e is silent, so say only "nol" | Say the word in a happy Christmas voice
exercise: How do you write école in capitals in good French? | ÉCOLE | ECOLE | ÈCOLE | EKOLE
exercise: What is the difference between sale and salé? | sale is "SAL" and means dirty; salé is "sa-LAY" and means salty | They are the same word in two spellings | sale means salty and salé means dirty | sale is a shop sale; salé is a salad
exercise: What is the name of the accent on è? | accent grave | accent aigu | accent circonflexe | cédille
exercise: What does Ce n'est pas grave ! mean? | It doesn't matter! | It isn't an accent grave! | It's not a grave! | It's not serious, call a doctor!
exercise: Which accent leans up to the right? | accent aigu, as in é | accent grave, as in è | tréma, as in ë | cédille, as in ç
test: Poppy moans, "cafe is literally the same as English, so unfair." What would café sound like without its é? | "KAF", because the final e would be silent | Exactly the same, so the accent is only decoration | "ka-FEE", like in an American diner | "ka-FAY", but only in capitals
test: Daniel in Liverpool asks, "Boss, so the dad's the sea?" ("Boss" means great.) What is the truth? | la mère is the mother; la mer is the sea | la mère is the sea; le père is the mayor | la mer is the dad, and le maire is the mum | They all mean the sea, just spelled differently
test: Hannah asks, "What's that wee hat on forêt for?" ("Wee" means small.) What is the best answer? | It shows an s that dropped out long ago | It shows that the word is about a king | It shows that the word must be shouted | It shows that the t is said very loud
test: Max says leaving off the cédille is "heaps unfair, it's just a squiggle." ("Heaps" means very.) What happens without it? | The c turns hard, so it sounds like "gar-KON" | Nothing changes, it is just a little squiggle | The c becomes silent, so it sounds like "gar-ON" | The word changes into the word for girl
test: Leah's mate says, "just put an accent on every a, it's safer." Why is that wrong? | a (has) and à (to, at) are different words | French has no accents on the letter a at all | Every a needs an accent, so the mate is right | Accents only go on the last letter of a word
test: Ollie wrote "Ou est la gare ?" His teacher laughs: "You asked 'or is the station'." What did he forget? | the accent grave on où | the cédille under the c | the two dots on the e | the capital letter on gare
test: Ben goes, "No way, capitals don't get accents." What would a French dictionary say? | Capitals keep their accents, as in ÉCOLE | Capitals never have accents in French | Only names in capitals get accents | Accents on capitals are against the law
test: Lily in Atlanta says, "Y'all, dirty and salty look the same!" Which pair is she looking at? | sale and salé | sel and selle | sale and sel | salé and salade
test: Alfie asks, "Them accents is well peng but what are they called?" ("Peng" means great.) Which name matches ç? | cédille | tréma | accent aigu | accent circonflexe
test: Camille writes, "Ce n'est pas grave !" after Ellie forgets the hat on gâteau. Ellie panics. What should she understand? | Camille is saying "no big deal", not talking about an accent | Camille is cross because Ellie forgot the accent grave | Camille wants Ellie to put a grave accent on the cake | Camille is saying the cake has been buried
test: ★★★ Using the lost-s rule, which English word matches the French word île? | isle | ill | eel | isn't
test: ★★★ A French word is written in four ways. Which is correct for "the boy is at the hospital"? | Le garçon est à l'hôpital. | Le garcon est a l'hopital. | Le garçon est a l'hôpital. | Le garçon est à l'hopitâl.
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of accents in written French? | They form an integral part of the spelling and may alter sound or meaning | They are purely decorative and may be omitted without consequence | They are employed solely upon words borrowed from English | They indicate exclusively that a syllable is to be stressed
exam: Identify the function of the cédille when placed beneath the letter c before a, o or u. | It causes the letter to be pronounced as "s" | It causes the letter to be pronounced as "k" | It renders the letter wholly silent | It causes the letter to be doubled
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the words ou and où. | The former signifies "or" and the latter "where", the two being pronounced identically | The former signifies "where" and the latter "or", the two being pronounced differently | Both signify "where", the accent being merely ornamental | Both signify "or", the accent indicating emphasis
exam: It is given that a circumflex accent frequently occupies the position of a letter lost from an older spelling. Determine the letter most commonly so replaced. | The letter s | The letter e | The letter h | The letter n
